# SundayStack — Fantasy Sports Platform

You are helping build **SundayStack**, a fantasy sports platform.

Long-term vision (do not build all of this now): Sleeper-style league hosting and drafts, ESPN-style league management, Fantasy Footballers-style research, and AI assistants (lineups, waivers, trades, draft, weekly summaries).

Initial goal: a working **NFL Fantasy Football MVP** using **free data sources and free infrastructure**.

See `docs/ROADMAP.md` for the current phase. See `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` and `docs/DATA_MODEL.md` before changing structure or schema.

**Current status:** Phase 0.5c done — 4-team playoffs (week 15 semis, week 16 championship; lazy bracket after week-14 REG games are `final`; trades close when seeds exist, 409 `PLAYOFFS`). Phase 0.5a FA/waivers, 0.5b trades, and Phase 0.4 weekly H2H points are done. Instant add is FA-window only (`WAIVER_PERIOD` during claims). Do not persist points on `player_game_stats`. Extra commissioner tools stay later in 0.5. Database is still Neon (Docker/Supabase unavailable locally; outbound 5432 blocked).

## Core Rule

The application must NEVER be tightly coupled to Sleeper, nflverse, SportsDataIO, ESPN, or any individual sports-data provider.

All external sports data must pass through a provider/normalization layer and be converted into our own internal data models:

```text
Sleeper / nflverse / SportsDataIO / Future Provider
      ↓
Sports Data Provider Layer
      ↓
Normalizer
      ↓
Our Internal Models
      ↓
PostgreSQL
      ↓
Fantasy Engine
```

Do not allow provider-specific response objects to leak outside their provider implementation.

Do not use Sleeper IDs (or any provider IDs) as primary application IDs. Every application object uses our UUID. External IDs live in mapping tables (`player_external_ids`, `team_external_ids`, `game_external_ids`, and later league mappings).

Sleeper is not our database. We import and normalize Sleeper data into our models.

## Technology Stack

```text
Language          TypeScript (strict). Avoid `any` unless absolutely necessary.
Web               Next.js, React, Tailwind CSS
API               Express.js on Node.js (all HTTP APIs live here; do not duplicate them in Next.js)
Mobile            React Native + Expo — later
Database          PostgreSQL. Intended platform is Supabase. Currently a free Neon project (Docker/Supabase unavailable locally; outbound 5432 blocked).
Auth              Express **dev login** (email + display name, no password) upserts stub `auth.users` + `public.users` so IDs stay `uuid = future auth.users.id`. Session via `SESSION_SECRET` (Bearer or httpOnly cookie). Replace with Supabase Auth later without changing league FKs. Public sports reads (`GET /api/players`, `GET /api/players/:id`, `GET /api/teams`) do not require auth. League, draft, matchup, waiver, and trade routes do (playoff bracket is lazy on scoreboard/standings GET). `PATCH /api/me` saves favorite NFL team + Home/Away jersey (not in the JWT).
Realtime          Draft board polls ~1.5s; scoreboard, waivers, and trades ~15s. Draft clock, week lineup lock, waiver process, trade expiry, and playoff bracket generation are lazy (no worker, Redis, or Supabase Realtime). WebSockets later if needed.
Cache             Upstash Redis later if needed
Hosting           Vercel (web). Express is local-first. Defaults are web 3000 / API 3001; this machine uses 3002 / 3010. CORS origin is `API_CORS_ORIGIN` — do not hardcode 3000. Next.js does not auto-load the repo-root `.env`; `apps/web/next.config.ts` must inject `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`.
Source control    GitHub
Repo / packages   sundaystack / @sundaystack/*
```

## Current MVP architecture

Do not scaffold the full long-term tree until that phase starts.

```text
sundaystack/
├── apps/web/                 # Next.js UI (login, leagues, roster, draft, matchup, waivers, trades, player, account)
├── apps/api/                 # Express REST (dev session + leagues + drafts + matchups + waivers + trades + public players/teams)
├── packages/shared/          # Domain types, scoring presets, lineup + snake-draft + schedule + waiver + trade + playoff rules, API DTOs
├── packages/fantasy-engine/  # Pure fantasy-point scoring (skill + K; DEF = 0)
├── packages/database/        # Drizzle schema + queries (sports + fantasy + drafts + matchups + waivers + trades + playoffs; DST seed CLI)
├── packages/sports-data/     # SportsDataProvider + nflverse + ingest CLI
├── supabase/migrations/      # Canonical SQL
└── docs/
```

Deferred until needed: `apps/mobile`, `packages/ui`, `packages/scoring`, `services/nfl-ingestion`, `services/scoring-worker`.

SQL migrations in `supabase/migrations` are canonical. Drizzle is a typed mirror — do not generate a second migration history with drizzle-kit.

`DATABASE_URL` is the app connection. Against Neon hosts, use the HTTP/serverless driver (port 443). Prefer `postgres.js` for local or Supabase direct (5432) connections. Do not commit `.env`.

Prefer simple solutions over premature microservices. Never make large architectural changes without explaining them. All HTTP APIs live in Express; do not add a parallel `/api` tree in Next.js.

## Sports data

Phase 0.1 primary source: **nflverse** (players, rosters, teams, schedules, weekly stats). Play-by-play, snaps, injuries, and advanced stats come later. Team DST is **not** in nflverse — seed 32 `players` with `position = DEF` and `player_external_ids.provider = sundaystack`. Kickers come from nflverse.

Sleeper is for later import of users/leagues/drafts/matchups — still normalized into our models.

Provider interface shape (extend with optional `getInjuries` / `getDepthCharts` when those phases start):

```typescript
interface SportsDataProvider {
  getPlayers(): Promise<NormalizedPlayer[]>;
  getTeams(): Promise<NormalizedTeam[]>;
  getSchedule(season: number, week?: number): Promise<NormalizedGame[]>;
  getPlayerStats(season: number, week?: number): Promise<NormalizedPlayerGameStats[]>;
}
```

Implementations: `NflverseProvider`, `MockProvider`, later `SleeperProvider`, `SportsDataIOProvider`.

## Scoring vs statistics

Sports providers supply real football statistics. **We** calculate fantasy points. Do not store fantasy points on `player_game_stats`. Do not hardcode league scoring into ingested stats.

The scoring engine (`packages/fantasy-engine`) currently implements Standard / Half PPR / Full PPR plus kicker FG/XP. DEF scores 0 until team-stat ingest. It must later support custom receptions, 4-pt/6-pt pass TDs, yardage/long-TD bonuses, TE premium, fractional and negative scoring, custom K/DST. Do not store fantasy points on `player_game_stats`.

## Database

Keep real-world sports data and fantasy application data logically separated. Phase 0.2–0.5c tables exist (`drafts`, `draft_order`, `draft_picks`, `draft_queues`, `matchups` with `kind` regular|playoff, `weekly_lineups`, `week_locks`, `waiver_priorities`, `faab_balances`, `waiver_periods`, `waiver_claims`, `trades`, `trade_players`, `playoff_seeds`). `users` has `first_name`, `last_name`, `avatar_url`, `favorite_team_id` + `jersey_side` (`home` | `away`). `teams` stores `primary_color` / `secondary_color` / `tertiary_color` (ingest). Waiver order is rolling from reverse draft order — not reverse standings. Playoff seeds snapshot standings once (wins then PF, then `teamId`); playoff games do not change regular W-L-T. 4-team single-elim on NFL **REG** weeks N+1 / N+2 (default 15–16); POST unused. Tie in a playoff game: higher seed advances. League status stays `active`. Trades are 2-team players only; accept swaps live `roster_players` immediately; trades close when playoff seeds exist (`PLAYOFFS`). FA/waivers keep the 0.5a calendar through playoff-week locks. Public `GET /api/players` stays unfiltered; `GET /api/teams` is the current 32 NFL franchises; the FA/waiver pool is a member-only league route. Extra commissioner tools stay later in 0.5. Player search UI is the roster/draft/waiver picker plus `/players/[id]`. Account (`/account`, signed-in only) is email, first/last name, profile photo, favorite NFL team, and Home/Away jersey kit (accent-driven CSS tokens). True Light/Dark stays later.

Use UUID PKs, `created_at`/`updated_at`, FKs, constraints, and indexes. Avoid derived data unless there is a performance reason (`players.team_id` as current team is an intentional denorm). Use JSONB only when it actually makes sense — not for major business objects.

## Coding rules

- Keep code modular. Put fantasy rules in domain/service layers, not UI.
- Avoid coupling UI to database records; use DTOs.
- Avoid duplicate business logic.
- Do not trust client-side validation for league rules; validate server-side.
- Consider concurrency for drafts, waivers, trades, roster changes, and playoff bracket inserts.
- Never expose provider API keys client-side. Secrets in environment variables.
- Useful comments only, not obvious ones.
- Keep README and `docs/` updated when architecture decisions change.

## Testing

Prioritize tests for: data normalization, scoring **presets**, roster/lineup validation, draft rules, fantasy **point** scoring, waivers, trades, standings, playoff seeding, jersey kits. Point scoring tests live in `packages/fantasy-engine`. Presets, lineup rules, snake/autopick, round-robin, waiver resolution, trade validation, playoff pairing, and Home/Away jersey tokens live in `packages/shared`.

## Cost

Target **$0/month**. Use free tiers. Do not introduce a paid API, database, host, auth, or monitoring product without discussing it first.

## UI (when we build UI)

Feel: Sleeper + modern sportsbook + fantasy analytics. Dark-mode friendly, clean, dense where useful, mobile-first. Avoid giant whitespace, excessive gradients, overly rounded chrome, generic AI SaaS look, unnecessary animation. All HTTP APIs stay on Express; the web app fetches `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` with credentials. Signed-in home is `/` (leagues if any, else join/create). Desktop: top Leagues + Account. Mobile: Fantasy + Account bottom tabs (session-only; hide on draft). In-league tabs: Draft or Match, Team, League (Players later). `/leagues` redirects to `/`.

## How to work

For each significant step: (1) what you found, (2) what you recommend, (3) which files change, (4) make the change, (5) how to test, (6) logical next step.

If uncertain, inspect existing code before assuming. Prefer incremental, testable changes.

Do not build large amounts of UI around a bad data model. Before a major feature: understand the domain → models → DB relationships → service boundaries → API contract → implement → test.
